# Can this mirror be right? Manet's A Bar at the Folies-Bergère
tags: ["manet","painting composition","perspective and reflection","viewer positioning","19th century art","realism and illusion"]
thumb: "question \"Can this mirror be right?\" picture \"File:Edouard_Manet,_A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Bergère.jpg\" colour"
teaches: ["art-history/manet-techniques","concepts/perspective-distortion","concepts/viewer-manipulation","methods/visual-narrative"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "Why doesn't the reflection match the barmaid? Who is the man in the mirror, and where are we?"

## A Bar at the Folies-Bergère: can this mirror be right?
sources:
    - id: courtauld
      title: A Bar at the Folies-Bergère
      authors: The Courtauld, London
      year: 2024
      finding: Completed a year before Manet died, shown at the 1882 Salon; Suzon posed in the studio; the customer's reflection and the bottles are shifted in the mirror; Bass beer with its red triangle
      url: https://courtauld.ac.uk/highlights/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere/
    - id: getty
      title: 'Manet''s Bar at the Folies-Bergère: the looking glass'
      authors: J. Paul Getty Museum (after Malcolm Park)
      year: 2007
      finding: Park's 2000 reconstruction shows the scene can be seen from a viewpoint to the right of the bar; the man stands outside the field of view and does not face her
      url: https://www.getty.edu/art/exhibitions/manet_bar/looking_glass.html
    - id: park
      title: Ambiguity, and the Engagement of Spatial Illusion within the Surface of Manet's Paintings
      authors: Malcolm Park
      year: 2001
      finding: Doctoral thesis (UNSW) with the photographic reconstruction of the bar

    card title "A Bar at the Folies-Bergère"

Our last painting asks a simple question: can this mirror be right?
    image art cites ["courtauld","getty"] credit "Édouard Manet, 1882. Courtauld Gallery, London. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 860 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Edouard_Manet%2C_A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Berg%C3%A8re.jpg/1920px-Edouard_Manet%2C_A_Bar_at_the_Folies-Berg%C3%A8re.jpg look screen regions.balcony [0,0.17,0.4,0.22] regions.barmaid [0.3,0,0.4,0.9] regions.bass [0.9,0.64,0.1,0.3] regions.behind_her [0.33,0,0.34,0.62] regions.champagne [0.12,0.62,0.15,0.32] regions.counter [0,0.62,1,0.38] regions.face [0.42,0.01,0.15,0.27] regions.front_bottles [0.02,0.63,0.25,0.31] regions.lamps [0.04,0.08,0.6,0.1] regions.liqueur [0.8,0.73,0.09,0.22] regions.man [0.87,0,0.13,0.45] regions.man_face [0.88,0.06,0.12,0.17] regions.mirror [0,0,1,0.78] regions.oranges [0.645,0.66,0.18,0.28] regions.refl_bottles [0.26,0.39,0.075,0.28] regions.reflection [0.69,0.1,0.29,0.6] regions.signature [0.022,0.79,0.05,0.1] regions.trapeze [0.02,0,0.06,0.085]

It is A Bar at the Folies-Bergère, the last big picture Édouard Manet finished, in 1882; he died the following year.
    portrait manet of "Édouard Manet" cameo true

    erase manet

A barmaid stands behind a marble counter in a Paris music hall, looking straight out at us.
    highlight art:barmaid

She was a real barmaid there, called Suzon, and she posed in Manet's studio behind a bar he set up for her.
    highlight art:face

In front of her: cha (@highlight art:champagne art:liqueur art:oranges art:bass@)mpagne, liqueur, a bowl of oranges, and English Bass beer with its red triangle.

Everything behind her is a huge mi (@highlight art:mirror art:balcony art:lamps strength soft@)rror: the glittering hall, the crowded balcony, the globe lamps.

    camera on ["art:trapeze"] zoom 3

Even the show is in it: up in the corner, a trapeze artist's green boots dangle into the picture.
    highlight art:trapeze

    camera reset true

    ask "Where should her reflection be? Tap the spot." spot behind_her on art
        intro: Now think about how a mirror works.
        right: Right behind her: with the mirror straight at her back, her own body would hide it.
        wrong: Stand with your back to a mirror and face someone: where would they see your back?

Instead, Manet slid her reflection far off to the right, as if the mirror stood at a slant.
    highlight art:reflection strength strong rest grey lift true

And the two don't agree: in front sh (@highlight art:barmaid art:reflection lift true@)e stands upright and keeps her distance; in the glass she leans in toward a customer.

    camera on ["art:man"] zoom 2.5

He is a man in a top hat, close enough to talk to her; the painter Gaston Latouche, a friend of Manet's, is said to have posed for him.
    highlight art:man_face

Here is the catch: if he stands right in front of her, he is standing exactly where we are.
    highlight art:man rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

So the mirror quietly casts us as that customer, and her blank gaze is aimed at him, and at us.
    highlight art:face lift true

    pause 0.5

    camera on ["art@[0.02,0.39,0.32,0.55]"] zoom 2

Even the bott (@highlight art:front_bottles art:refl_bottles@)les on the left don't match their reflections, and people have argued ever since whether Manet blundered or meant it.

    camera reset true

Then, in 2000, the art historian Malcolm Park photographed a staged bar from off to the right, and the reflection appeared, the man off to the side, not face to face; others still say Manet bent it on purpose.
    highlight art:reflection art:man strength soft

    camera on ["art:signature"] zoom 6

Like van Eyck writing on the wall, Manet signs inside the scene: Manet, 1882, on a bottle's label.
    highlight art:signature

    camera reset true

So the picture holds two moments at once: the woman as she faces us,
    focus art:face strength strong rest blur

and the woman as a customer sees her.
    focus art:reflection strength strong rest blur

A mirror that ho (@highlight art:mirror art:counter art:man lift true@)lds the whole room, a viewpoint you cannot quite find, a watcher who turns out to be you: van Eyck's little mirror has grown to fill the wall.

    quiz "A filmmaker wants viewers to feel they are a character in the scene, as Manet does. Which shot follows his method?"
        right: The reflection: it puts someone in the camera's place, so the viewer becomes that stranger, as we become Manet's customer.
        wrong: Being looked at is only half of it: who else does Manet put where we stand?
        * A wide shot of the whole room
        * The actor stares straight into the lens
        + A reflection shows someone standing where the camera is
        * A close-up of the actor's tears

Can this mirror be right? Not quite, and that is exactly how it pulls you up to the bar.
    highlight art:face lift true

